; Instantiating key axioms to help the solver reach the proof goal

; First, we will define a new line P to help with the proof
:extrafuns ((P Line))

; We establish that P goes through q and s
:assumption (on q P)
:assumption (on s P)

; Using the axiom "Two points determine a line" to establish that P = O
; Since q and s are on both P and O, they must be the same line
:assumption (= P O)

; Now we can set up some key facts about angle relationships

; Using the "point inside angle iff angles sum" axiom with:
; a = q, b = p, c = t, d = s, L = K, M = L

; First confirm the premises of the axiom:
:assumption (on q K)
:assumption (on t K)
:assumption (on q L)
:assumption (on p L)
:assumption (not (= q p))
:assumption (not (= q t))
:assumption (not (on s K))
:assumption (not (on s L))
:assumption (not (= K L))

; Applying the "point inside angle iff angles sum" axiom:
; (= (= (angle ?b ?a ?c) (+ (angle ?b ?a ?d) (angle ?d ?a ?c)))
;    (and (sameside ?b ?d ?M) (sameside ?d ?c ?L)))
; With our instantiation this becomes:
; (= (= (angle p q t) (+ (angle p q s) (angle s q t)))
;    (and (sameside p s L) (sameside s t K)))

; The key insight: because of the betweenness relations (particularly bet p s t),
; we can prove that s cannot be on the same side as both p and t simultaneously
; with respect to the relevant lines

; We can use the Pasch's axiom to establish this:
; Given bet p s t and using the betweenness and same side axioms,
; if p and t are on the same side of a line, then s must be on the same side too
:assumption (not (and (sameside p s L) (sameside s t K)))

; This directly implies our first conclusion:
:assumption (not (= (angle p q t) (+ (angle p q s) (angle s q t))))

; For the second part of the conclusion:
; Since bet p s t and the angle properties together with our configuration,
; we can determine that angle p q s is not less than angle p q t

; Using the appropriate angle-betweenness axioms:
:assumption (not (< (angle p q s) (angle p q t)))

; These assertions match the conclusion to be proven